{
  "subtitle-win": [
    "Spain and France lead the tightly bunched field for the 2026 FIFA World Cup title, with several other European sides and South American powers close behind, reflecting deep squads, strong recent qualifying campaigns, and the expanded 48-team format that rewards consistency across multiple matches. Lamine Yamal's hamstring recovery has helped stabilize Spain's position, while France benefits from defensive organization under Didier Deschamps despite forward absences like Hugo Ekitike. Argentina's Lionel Messi manages hamstring concerns, Brazil contends with key injuries including Rodrygo, and England maintains momentum amid roster decisions. These factors, combined with home-soil advantages for the co-hosts and the wisdom of crowds pricing in historical performance and current form, keep probabilities clustered as teams finalize preparations ahead of the June 11 kickoff."
  ],
  "subtitle-final": [
    "With the 2026 FIFA World Cup opening in days across North America, trader focus on nations reaching the final centers on Spain and France as consensus frontrunners due to their depth, recent Nations League and qualifying results, and star availability. Spain regained solo-favorite status after positive updates on Lamine Yamal\u2019s hamstring recovery, while France benefits from its balanced roster and strong recent form. Defending champions Argentina, alongside England and Brazil, remain competitive given their attacking talent and historical knockout success, though the expanded 48-team format increases path uncertainty through group stage and knockout rounds. Home advantages for co-hosts and potential upsets from sides like Morocco or Portugal add volatility to implied probabilities."
  ],
